# mounts.conf — which host directories this machine's sandbox can see.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE EXAMPLE. Copy it to `mounts.conf` and edit that:
#
#     cp mounts.conf.example mounts.conf
#     ./install_quadlet.sh
#
# `mounts.conf` is gitignored. It is per-machine, per-project
# configuration. Airlock itself is project-agnostic, so no project's
# paths belong in the repo - this file is the ONLY place a real path is
# ever named, and it is never committed.
#
# FORMAT — one mount per line:
#
#     <host path>:<container path>[:<mode>]
#
#   host path       absolute, or ~/... . Must exist at install time.
#   container path  absolute, where it appears inside the container.
#   mode            ro (default) or rw. Leave it off unless a run must
#                   WRITE to the host tree — /out exists for products,
#                   and read-only is what makes "the runner cannot
#                   damage the original" true rather than hoped.
#
# Blank lines and # comments are ignored.
#
# The agent lane mounts (/drop, /out, /logs, /status) are NOT listed
# here. They are the mechanism itself, not configuration, so they stay
# in quadlet/sandbox-runner.container.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Examples. Delete these and write your own.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# A project to run tests against, read-only:
# ~/code/MyProject:/projects/MyProject

# A large read-only corpus shared by several projects. Keep such trees
# out of per-project mounts so they are fetched and stored once:
# ~/code/Sources:/sources

# A scratch tree the runner may write to (rare — prefer /out):
# ~/code/Scratch:/scratch:rw

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sizing note, learned the hard way: do NOT mount a home directory or
# anything containing multi-gigabyte trees wholesale. A recursive walk
# over one of those exhausts file descriptors and wedges the caller.
# Mount the specific subdirectory a run actually needs.
